<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Lament · 1840 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>A Scottish folk song about the lovely shores of Loch Lomond, often linked with the Jacobite rising of 1745.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>Scots</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>English (with Scots words)</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>Scotland</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>often dated to 1746 and linked with the Jacobite rising of 1745; printed by 1840</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Lament</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional; one of the earliest printings in 1840)</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a>&quot;, or simply &quot;Loch Lomond&quot;, is a traditional Scottish folk song (Roud 9598), named after the largest loch in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>; in Scots &quot;bonnie&quot; means fair or beautiful. Its maker is unknown. It has often been dated to 1746, and its origins are thought to lie in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jacobite-rising-of-1745">Jacobite rising of 1745</a>, with which it has long been associated.</p><p>Two readings of the words are common. In one, it is a lament of the march of December 1745, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-edward-stuart">Bonnie Prince Charlie</a> retreated into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, sung by a soldier who rues his sacrifice for the king; in Celtic mythology the &quot;low road&quot; was a fairy route by which the soul of a soldier killed abroad returned home. In the other, the chorus gives the last words of a Jacobite rebel before his execution, perhaps at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlisle">Carlisle</a>, where dozens of rebels were executed: seeing his lover at the gallows, he tells her they will meet again in Scotland, though by different roads. In spite of these Jacobite roots, the song became one of a handful of folk songs that stood for British unity as the empire grew.</p><p>One of the earliest <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/printing-press">printings</a> of the music was published in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a> in 1840 by Paterson as &quot;Bonnie Loch Loman&quot;, credited to &quot;a lady&quot;, with arrangements by Finlay Dunn and John Thomson. Lady John Scott was often named as its composer, but she only wrote down the tune and words after hearing a boy sing them in the streets of Edinburgh; in William Black's novel Wild Eelin (1898) the heroine, Eelin MacDonald, dismisses as &quot;spurious&quot; the idea that an Edinburgh street urchin would ever sing it. Both the 1840 music and Lady John Scott's words differ a good deal from the form sung today. A broadside of 1849, &quot;Flora's Lament for Her Charlie&quot;, largely parallels its words; Flora MacDonald, a supporter of the prince, was imprisoned for helping him escape his pursuers. There are local variants too, among them one that sets the lovers' parting in Luss glen. The song is often wrongly credited to the Scottish poet Andrew Lang, who wrote a poem titled &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/the-bonnie-banks-o-loch-lomond">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond</a>, 1746&quot; in 1876; it names Sergeant Môr, John Du Cameron, a Jacobite who fought on as an outlaw until he was captured and hanged in 1753. The Irish song &quot;Red Is the Rose&quot;, popularised by Tommy Makem, has the same melody and different words on a similar theme; it is traditional, not Makem's own.</p><p>The tune has precedents in other folk songs: the Danish and Faroese &quot;Dankonning lod gribe en havfrue fin&quot; (&quot;The Dane-King Captured a Mermaid&quot;), &quot;Godesses&quot; in John Playford's The Dancing Master (1651), which &quot;The Oak and the Ash&quot; also resembles, and fragments of &quot;The Bonniest Lass in a' the World&quot; and &quot;The Bonnie House of Airlie&quot;. It has often been described as sharing a melody with &quot;Kind Robin Loes Me&quot; and its variants, such as &quot;Robin Cushie&quot;.</p><p>Composers took it up early. Ferdinand Thieriot wrote a symphonic fantasy on the tune in 1868, Charles Ives quoted it in &quot;The Alcotts&quot; movement of his Concord Sonata, and Ralph Vaughan Williams arranged it for baritone and male choir in 1921. Marie Narelle recorded it for Edison in 1906 and Harry Lauder in 1926. Maxine Sullivan's swing version, arranged by Claude Thornhill and recorded on 6 August 1937, made her career and led many others to swing folk songs; she recorded it 14 times, the last at the Fujitsu-Concord Jazz Festival in Tokyo in 1986. Benny Goodman's orchestra played Thornhill's arrangement, sung by Martha Tilton, at its Carnegie Hall concert of 1938.</p><p>The Scottish band Runrig recorded it in 1979 and made it their first single in 1982; in 2007 they recorded it again with 50,000 members of the Tartan Army, the supporters of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>'s football team, for the BBC's Children in Need, and the single went to number 1 in the Scottish chart. AC/DC released a rock version, &quot;Fling Thing&quot;, in 1976 and played it at the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/apollo">Apollo</a> <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">Theatre</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a>. The song is also the anthem of FC Köln, whose fans sing it before each match. In films and television it often marks a Scottish setting or character, from Laurel and Hardy's Tit for Tat to the Scottish terrier Jock in Disney's Lady and the Tramp (1955).</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of parting by the loch: the singer remembers the bright shores of Loch Lomond where he and his true love walked, and knows that he will be in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> before her, she by the high road and he by the low, though they will never meet there again. Sung slowly it is a lament of loss and exile, with the loch's beauty set against the sorrow; its well-loved chorus has also carried it into marches, swing and the football terraces.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-1"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">The words</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>By yon bonnie banks and by yon bonnie braes,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where the sun shines bright on Loch Lomond,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where me and my true love were ever wont to gae,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On the bonnie, bonnie banks o' Loch Lomond.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>O ye'll tak' the high road, and I'll tak' the low road,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And I'll be in Scotland afore ye,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But me and my true love will never meet again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On the bonnie, bonnie banks o' Loch Lomond.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>'Twas there that we parted, in yon shady glen,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>On the steep, steep side o' Ben Lomond,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Where in soft purple hue, the highland hills we view,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And the moon coming out in the gloaming.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>The wee birdies sing and the wildflowers spring,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>And in sunshine the waters are sleeping.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>But the broken heart it kens nae second spring again,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" data-say>Though the waeful may cease frae their grieving.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Words: Traditional (the common text, as quoted on English Wikipedia). Text from <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/The_Bonnie_Banks_o'_Loch_Lomond" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikipedia</a>; public domain.</p></div></section>
</div>
<section class="sg-sec" id="listen"><h2>Listen</h2><div class="sg-video" data-yt="tU4kJ6tIDao" data-title="The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond: Runrig, the Scottish band named in the article"><button type="button" class="sg-play"><span class="sg-play-ic" aria-hidden="true"></span><span class="sg-play-t">Play the performance</span><span class="sg-play-n">Runrig, the Scottish band named in the article</span></button></div><p class="sg-video-cr">On YouTube, from the channel Runrig - Topic. Pressing play loads YouTube&#8217;s player. <a href="https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=tU4kJ6tIDao" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Watch on YouTube<span aria-hidden="true"> &#8599;</span></a></p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Flora's Lament for Her Charlie</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1849</span><span class="sg-chain-h">broadside largely paralleling the words</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ferdinand Thieriot, symphonic fantasy on the tune</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1868</span><span class="sg-chain-h">fantasy</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Bonnie Banks o' Loch Lomond, 1746 (Andrew Lang)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1876</span><span class="sg-chain-h">poem of the same title</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Wild Eelin (William Black)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1898</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the heroine's own words to it in a novel</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Concord Sonata, &quot;The Alcotts&quot; (Charles Ives)</span><span class="sg-chain-h">quoted in</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Ralph Vaughan Williams, arrangement for baritone and male choir</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1921</span><span class="sg-chain-h">arrangement</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Maxine Sullivan, arranged by Claude Thornhill</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1937</span><span class="sg-chain-h">swing version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Benny Goodman and His Orchestra with Martha Tilton, The Famous 1938 Carnegie Hall Jazz Concert</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1938</span><span class="sg-chain-h">swing performance</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Lady and the Tramp (film)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1955</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new words sung by Jock</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Fling Thing (AC/DC)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1976</span><span class="sg-chain-h">rock version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Runrig</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1982</span><span class="sg-chain-h">single; again with the Tartan Army in 2007</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Rhythm of My Heart (Marc Jordan and John Capek)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1986</span><span class="sg-chain-h">melody reworked in the chorus</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Red Is the Rose</span><span class="sg-chain-h">Irish song on the same melody</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dankonning lod gribe en havfrue fin</span><span class="sg-chain-h">a precedent for the tune</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Godesses (John Playford, The Dancing Master)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1651</span><span class="sg-chain-h">the tune resembles it</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Bonniest Lass in a' the World</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1733</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar melodic fragments</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">The Bonnie House of Airlie</span><span class="sg-chain-h">similar melodic fragments</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Kind Robin Loes Me</span><span class="sg-chain-h">often described as the same melody</span></li></ul></section>
